@mgol generally the ESM version would only be used by bundlers right? It might be a good start to simply only have the `module` field in package json and in the npm module but not add this to CDNs

If people end up making use of this those might be good steps to take in the future? 

If you are on board with this approach, I would be happy to give ESM a shot.

As far as other packages, yes, the plugin architecture ought to be changed to support something like:

```js
export default function ($) {
    $.fn.greenify = function() {
        this.css( "color", "green" );
        return this;    
    };
    return $;
}
```

...so that calling code could instead do:

```js
// Or using `node_modules` paths for these, noting that such might not work in some cases, e.g.,
//   plugins importing other plugins, since `node_modules` is not always flattened.
import jQuery from 'jquery';
import myPlugin from 'myPlugin';
import anotherPlugin from 'anotherPlugin';

anotherPlugin(myPlugin(jQuery));

// Or optionally, this:

// const $ = anotherPlugin(myPlugin(jQuery));
```

here's my solve for it, using a CDN ( esm.sh is nice because you can self-host it if you like - took about 30m to get a docker container up and running with it).

```

// NOTE:  need to include jquery, *then* jquery-ui, *then* bootstrap, etc.
import $ from 'https://esm.sh/jquery'

async function main() {
  // these have global dependency on $/jQuery, sigh
  window.jQuery = $
  window.$ = $
  // import all in parallel
  await Promise.all([
    import('https://esm.sh/tablesorter'),
    import('https://esm.sh/slick-carousel@1.8.1'),
    import('https://esm.sh/v53/jqueryui@1.11.1/es2021/jquery-ui.min.js'),
    import('https://esm.sh/jquery-ui-touch-punch'),
    import('https://esm.sh/bootstrap@5.1.3'),
  ])
}

(async () => await main())()
```

@gnat I don’t see anything ESM-related in that PR. In order to expose a library as an ECMAScript Module, you need to use the `export` syntax which is not supported in regular scripts. It’s impossible to handle both in a single file.

Exporting as CommonJS may make it possible to import from a file if you use a bundler that supports it, which is pretty common. But it does nothing to support actual native ESM.

jQuery already exposes itself via AMD, CommonJS & browser globals.
